(vector
 (map
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112600-counting-a-name-is-not-counting-uses-of-a-mechanism")
  (:adr/title "Counting a name is not counting uses of a mechanism")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609112400-payload-v3-is-one-rewrite-and-one-decision"))
  (:adr/body "
## The correction

The previous iteration recorded dimension 2 -- whether (i64 5) and 5 stay
one identity -- as payload v3's only non-mechanical part, and described
the cost of unifying as: the explicit i64 wrapper cannot round-trip.

That framing treated the wrapper as something a user writes. It is not.
Measured 2026-09-11: the compiler puts EVERY host integer through it, on
both hosts. `kotoba.compiler.definition-identity/canonical-value` is
handed to the alpha-normalization walk as `:scalar`, so it reaches every
scalar leaf of every definition, and its own docstring gives the reason --
it erases the one place where the JVM holds a long and nbb holds a
BigInt, and it is free ONLY because the two normalize to the same bytes.

So the unification is not a convenience to be traded away. It is
load-bearing for the compiler, and a v3 that splits on the wrapper does
not move one fixture, it changes every integer in every definition.

## How the count misled me

A first pass grepped for the wrapper keyword and found three occurrences
in osaho and ZERO in amu, and read that as: the mechanism is exercised
only by its own tests. The opposite is true. amu never spells the keyword
because it CONSTRUCTS the wrapper through the constructor, so a search for
the literal finds the one repository that defines it and misses the one
that uses it everywhere.

Counting occurrences of a name is not counting uses of a mechanism.

## The live defect this found

The conversion payload v3 needs already exists, written ahead of need in
osaho's payload-v3-unwrap test and deliberately kept in test/ because
putting it in src/ would require promoting io-ipld and thereby take the v3
decision by accident.

Its i64 branch was unconditional: every wrapper became the value model's
exact integer. And the only test that reached that branch used an
out-of-range value -- the single case where unconditional is right. The
conversion was tested exactly where it happened to be correct.

## Measured

Each of the ten frozen payloads, shaped the way the compiler shapes one
(every integer wrapped), encoded both ways:

    drafted    261 273 296 261 261 280 387 702 275 264
    magnitude  221 233 256 221 221 240 347 614 243 232

Eight bytes per integer. The magnitude column is byte-identical to the
same payload with no wrappers at all, and that equality is the property
worth naming: wrapping every integer costs nothing, which is why the
compiler does it, and the conversion has to preserve it.

Control: a payload containing no integers shows delta 0.

An earlier run of this comparison showed delta 0 for every vector, which
looked like the fix doing nothing. It was correct -- the FIXTURES carry
bare integers, not wrappers, so there was almost nothing for either
conversion to act on. The measurement was right and the expectation about
its input was wrong. A payload has to be shaped the way the compiler
shapes one before it can show this at all.

## Reproduce

    cd <osaho worktree>
    kbb -M:test -n kotoba.kir.payload-v3-unwrap-test

## Seen red

Restoring the unconditional branch fails 18 assertions across both new
tests. The boundary is exact on purpose: max-safe-integer must stay
compact and max+1 must not, because a comparison with no value on the line
cannot show which operator it is using.

## A control of mine that was wrong

The first version asserted that bare and wrapped encode DIFFERENTLY beyond
the exact range. They do not merely differ -- the bare form cannot be
encoded at all, and the codec refuses it. That is a stronger fact and it
is the reason the wrapper exists. The refusal literal is now pinned rather
than the inequality.

## Where this leaves the decision

v3 must decide by MAGNITUDE, not by the presence of the wrapper. That
keeps normalize's unification, keeps the compiler's canonicalization free,
and spends the exact-integer code only where the value model actually
needs it.

Dimensions 1 and 3 remain a rewrite that moves every DefCID. What changed
is that dimension 2 is no longer an open question with two defensible
answers: one of them is now measured to be wrong.

## Not claimed

io-ipld stays test-only. The conversion still lives in test/ and nothing
here promotes it.

## Noticed, not fixed

osaho main carries two pre-existing failures in
kir_bit_or_typed_admission_test (the i64 shift family). They are on the
clean baseline as well as after this change, and are a separate concern.
")))
